{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If by himself.</b> Some say בגפו is like בגופו, and we find גופת שאול (I Chronicles 10:12), and the patterns of nouns vary. Others said it is like כנף, and כנף in the language of Targum is וגפין, as if he said 'with the edge of his garment alone' — but this is not from the meaning of the matter, for its interpretation should only be that no other body is dependent upon him. And so it will be like על גבי חרשו חורשים (Psalms 129:3). The Gaon [Saadia] said: Scripture needed to say 'if by himself he came, by himself he shall go out,' just as it says 'he shall go out free' on account of the matter of his wife — for it was necessary to say 'if he is a husband.' And since an Israelite woman is not sold for her theft by a court, what reason is there to say 'and his wife shall go out with him'? To inform that the one who acquires a Hebrew slave must provide for his wife, and she shall stay with him until they both go out together."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "Resolved הגאון as the Gaon [Saadia] per standard convention; the repetitive phrasing of the biblical verses is compressed in Ibn Ezra's argument and preserved as-is."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:37.661998010Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}